A named derivation an author can edit: a label a reader understands, and an Expression field that produces the value.

## Usage

```ts
import { FormulaField } from '@cardstack/catalog/fields/formula/formula-field';

@field annualised = contains(FormulaField);
```

## The owner passes itself

A FieldDef cannot reach the card holding it, so evaluation takes the subject as an argument. The owning card supplies itself at the one call site:

```ts
@field projectedTotal = contains(NumberField, {
  computeVia: function (this: Invoice) {
    return this.annualised?.valueFor(this);
  },
});
```

`valueFor(subject)` returns the value or `undefined`. `errorFor(subject)` returns the message from an evaluation that actually ran, which is the one to show an author — `status` reports compilation only, and an expression that compiles can still resolve nothing.

## Reading status

- **Empty** — no expression written yet
- **Compiles** — the source parses; it may still fail against a given subject
- **Error** — the source does not parse

The deliberate gap between "compiles" and "works" is why `status` says Compiles rather than Ready. A formula is only proven against a subject, and the field has no subject until someone hands it one.
